---
import ProjectLayout from '../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import CaseImage from '../../../components/CaseImage.astro';
import zabbixMonitoring from '../../../assets/case-studies/zabbix-monitoring.png';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Observability and support SLA"
  description="REST API integration between the helpdesk system and Grafana for real-time SLA tracking, replacing manual ticket review."
  domain="observability"
  caseId="05"
>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    The support team had no real-time visibility into SLA compliance — tracking relied on manual
    ticket review in the helpdesk system, which delayed awareness of violations until they had
    already impacted the customer.
  </p>

  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <p>
    Consuming the helpdesk system's (GLPI) REST API to extract ticket data — opening time, status
    changes, priority, first-response time, and resolution time — and modeling Grafana dashboards
    with that data. SLA violation alerts were automatically routed to Jira and Microsoft Teams,
    reusing the same flow already used for infrastructure alerts coming from Zabbix.
  </p>

  <CaseImage
    src={zabbixMonitoring}
    alt="List of monitored items in Zabbix for a host, showing CPU, memory, swap, and filesystem metrics with real-time values"
    caption="Monitored items in Zabbix — the same infrastructure alerting layer repurposed to feed the SLA pipeline. Hostname removed."
  />

  <h2>Technical challenges</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Data normalization</strong> — older tickets had inconsistent status fields or missing transition
      timestamps, requiring cleanup before feeding the dashboards.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Defining relevant metrics</strong> — not every ticket carries the same weight; SLA metrics
      had to be segmented by priority level instead of using a general average, which masked violations
      in critical tickets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Avoiding alert fatigue</strong> — the first automated alerts generated too much noise; thresholds
      were tuned to trigger only close to the actual SLA limit, not on every status change.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <p>
    Real-time SLA visibility for support leadership, with automated alerts replacing manual review,
    and historical data consistent enough for data-driven conversations about staffing.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>
